WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.280
In 1977 a drought hit Daphne Major, a small island in the Galápagos.

2
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.840
About 85 percent of its medium ground finches died.

3
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.880
The birds that lived were not a random sample of the ones that died.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.500
So will their children be different?

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.300
Not yet. Survival is only the first half of the answer.

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.993
Here is a made-up population.

7
00:00:29.853 --> 00:00:32.753
The numbers are illustrative, not the Grants' measurements.

8
00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:38.473
Before the drought, mean beak depth is 9.40 millimetres.

9
00:00:39.213 --> 00:00:42.793
The birds that survive and breed average 9.80.

10
00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:47.513
Subtract, and the gap is 0.40 millimetres.

11
00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:53.393
This is the selection differential, S. It is selection within one generation.

12
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:57.667
But the survivors' children are the next generation.

13
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.227
Selection says who lived.

14
00:01:00.787 --> 00:01:03.587
It says nothing yet about what those children look like.

15
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.207
For that, you need heritability.

16
00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:11.867
Narrow-sense heritability is the part of a trait's variation that makes offspring

17
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:13.187
resemble their parents.

18
00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:18.387
It belongs to one population in one environment, not to the trait everywhere.

19
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:21.867
The breeder's equation joins the two.

20
00:01:22.627 --> 00:01:32.967
The response, R, equals h² times S. Assume h² is 0.60, then R is

21
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:39.027
0.60 times 0.40. That is 0.24 millimetres.

22
00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:43.327
So the predicted offspring mean is 9.64.

23
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:50.177
If heritability were zero, R would be zero, however strong the selection.

24
00:01:51.157 --> 00:01:54.957
The survivors' children would look like the population before the drought.

25
00:01:56.017 --> 00:01:57.697
The direction can also flip.

26
00:01:58.677 --> 00:02:05.337
Suppose a later drought, in a population again averaging 9.40, leaves survivors

27
00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:13.817
at 9.20. Then S is minus 0.20, and R is minus 0.12.

28
00:02:14.737 --> 00:02:17.837
The predicted offspring mean is 9.28.

29
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.440
S happens within a generation.

30
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.460
R happens across generations.

31
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.980
Only R is evolution.

32
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.580
And this R is a prediction.

33
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.000
Each estimate belongs to one population, one environment and one set of years.

34
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.080
Only real offspring can test it.

35
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.820
So when survivors differ, ask one more question.

36
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.740
Were the offspring measured?
